LESSON: Tank fill valve control

LESSON: Tank fill valve control
1.  **Float Switch Logic**: In Structured Text (ST) for PLCs, float switches are typically active-low (they close the circuit to ground when submerged). We need to read the state of the low and high float switches.
2.  **Valve Control**: The fill valve should be OPEN (HIGH) when the low float is ON (indicating the tank is empty) and CLOSED (LOW) when the high float is ON (indicating the tank is full).
3.  **Implementation**: We will use a simple `IF` statement to check the low float condition. If the low float is active, the valve opens. If the high float is active, the valve closes.
4.  **Safety**: It is good practice to ensure the valve closes if the high float is detected, regardless of the low float state, to prevent overfilling.

Here is the Structured Text code:

MAKE CODE: Tank Fill Valve Control
language st
```st
// Tank Fill Valve Control
// Low Float Switch: Active Low (ON when tank is empty)
// High Float Switch: Active Low (ON when tank is full)
// Fill Valve: Output (HIGH = Open, LOW = Closed)

// Input variables (assuming digital inputs)
VAR
  lowFloat : BOOL;
  highFloat : BOOL;
END_VAR

// Output variable
VAR
  fillValve : BOOL;
END_VAR

// Logic: Open valve if low float is ON (tank empty)
// Close valve if high float is ON (tank full)
// Note: This assumes the float switches are active-low.
// If the switches are active-high, invert the logic here.
fillValve := lowFloat AND NOT highFloat;

// Optional: Ensure valve closes if high float is detected even if low float is also detected
// The above logic handles this, but explicit safety check can be added if needed.
// fillValve := lowFloat AND NOT highFloat;
```

END
